\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Comparison with the anomalous Jacobian}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Lüscher uses a half-shift on both fields in an infinitesimal
axial flavor-singlet transformation. The measure variation in
his Ward identity gives #{-a\trace(\Gamma D)}, which he relates
to the trace of chirality on the spectral subspace at zero
\citet{Section 4, Eqs. (4.1), (4.3), (4.6)–(4.7), printed pp. 3–4}{luscher1998exact}.
Kikukawa and Yamada give a one-sided infinitesimal variation.
Their unbarred field transforms by #{\Gamma'=\Gamma(\bbI -aD)}
and their barred field by right multiplication with #{\Gamma}
\citet{Eq. (12), p. 5}{kikukawa1999axial}.}

\p{For the complex Ginsparg–Wilson operator and a unit #{u} of
modulus one, the finite inverse coefficient pullback in
\ref{fdip-014L} has phase #{u^{-2\,\sdim\ker D}}.
With right chirality positive, this kernel index counts right
minus left. Hasenfratz, Laliena and Niedermayer count left
minus right, expressing that index by a trace with their
blocking kernel and by their fixed-point topological charge
\citet{Eqs. (10)–(12), p. 4}{hasenfratz1998index}.}

\p{For Weyl fermions in three space dimensions and one time
dimension, Hsieh obtains anomaly-cancellation conditions on
their cyclic charges. The two symmetry groups are
#{\operatorname{Spin}(4)\times\mathbb Z_n} and
#{(\operatorname{Spin}(4)\times\mathbb Z_{2m})/\mathbb Z_2},
with the diagonal subgroup in the quotient
\citet{Section 1, Eqs. (1.2)–(1.3), printed pp. 2–3}{hsieh2018discrete}.}
